<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.Recommendations</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.Recommendations</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Recommendation &amp; Personalization Platform (Phase B14). The platform's single owner of
      the question "what should this particular member see next?".

      This is not a recommendation table. It is the intelligence layer between "the platform knows
      things" and "this member sees something", and the distinction is the whole design: every other
      module publishes facts about what exists and what happened, while this module knows nothing
      about the facts and everything about members, signals, affinities, policies and ranking.

      What Recommendations owns: the immutable signal record, the derived personalization profile,
      the member's explicit personalization preferences, the generated recommendation set with its
      explanations and score breakdowns, the feedback record, the ranking-strategy catalogue, the
      recommendation-policy catalogue, and the analytics rollups. What it deliberately does not own:
      any business data. There is no job, listing, guide, event or provider in this schema — only a
      candidate projection assembled from published contracts, which is a shadow of somebody else's
      truth and is rebuildable by replaying the outbox. Delete the whole schema and nothing is lost
      but personalization quality.

      Business modules never compute recommendations. They publish integration events, and this
      module consumes them. A ranking strategy and a recommendation policy are reference data, not
      code paths, so changing "trust weighs more than recency on the housing surface" is a catalogue
      change rather than a deployment.

      Ranking is deterministic and explainable. Every recommendation carries a reason code, a score,
      the strategy that produced it and the signals it was derived from; there is no opaque path
      through this module. Machine learning, vector search, embeddings, semantic similarity and LLM
      ranking are explicitly out of scope and each has an abstraction shaped for it —
      IRankingStrategy, ISimilarityProvider, IRecommendationCandidateSource — with a deterministic
      implementation behind it today and no vendor named anywhere in the assembly.

      Registers entirely through B2.6 contributor interfaces: no edit to the Platform Kernel, to any
      previously completed module, or to Program.cs.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The same narrow reference set B13 established, and for the same reason. Recommendations consumes
    published contracts and nothing else, so it references Diaspora.Contracts for the events it
    subscribes to and takes no ProjectReference on Trust, Community, Guides, Jobs, Marketplace,
    Housing, Community Life, Providers, Notifications or Location at all. A recommended job is
    assembled entirely from the payload of JobPublishedIntegrationEvent; if that payload is not
    enough, the fix is to enrich the contract, never to reach into the module.

    Diaspora.Application is referenced for the CQRS abstractions and the integration-event handler
    base; Diaspora.Persistence for IDatabaseContributor and the shared context. Architecture tests
    enforce the absence of every module reference.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The consumer-side vocabulary maps — the two internal classes that translate another module's
    content-type and subject-type strings into this module's target types — are the one thing in here
    worth testing directly and the one thing that should not be public. A wrong entry silently files a
    boost or a bookmark against the wrong kind of thing, which no integration test would notice.
    The same arrangement Diaspora.Operations and Diaspora.Persistence already use.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Diaspora.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.SharedKernel\Diaspora.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Contracts\Diaspora.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Platform\Diaspora.Platform.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Application\Diaspora.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Persistence\Diaspora.Persistence.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
